  <!-- ════════ THE INTERACTIVE ════════ -->
  <section class="dp-calc" id="explore">
    <div class="dp-calc-head">
      <h2 class="dp-h2">If &mdash; or when &mdash; it returns to trend&hellip;</h2>
      <span class="dp-readout" id="dpHorizonReadout">&mdash;</span>
    </div>

    <p class="dp-cue">Drag to choose <strong>how fast</strong> the return happens. Nothing here predicts that it will &mdash; the speed is <em>your</em> assumption, and the arithmetic below is its consequence. Fast reversions look extreme because they <strong>are</strong> extreme. It has happened before: from the Nov 2022 low (0.41&times; trend), price was back at trend within about 16 months.</p>

    <div class="dp-slider-block">
      <div class="dp-slider-wrap">
        <input type="range" min="6" max="60" step="1" value="36" class="dp-slider" id="dpSlider"
               aria-label="Years for price to revert to trend, from six months to five years">
      </div>
      <!-- Record markers (fastest / median / longest time-to-trend), labelled + placed live by JS (§9 Phase 4). -->
      <div class="dp-dur-marks" id="dpDurMarks" hidden></div>
      <div class="dp-slider-scale"><span>6 months &middot; fastest</span><span>2.5 years</span><span>5 years &middot; slowest</span></div>
      <p class="dp-slider-caption">A shorter horizon means the <strong>same gap closes in less time</strong>, so the implied CAGR is higher. That is just arithmetic, not a forecast.</p>
      <p class="dp-dur-slidercap" id="dpDurSliderCap" hidden></p>
    </div>

