  <!-- ════════ THE HONESTY BACKTEST ════════ -->
  <section class="dp-section" id="backtest">
    <div class="dp-eyebrow">The test any &ldquo;cheap or expensive&rdquo; claim should pass</div>
    <h2 class="dp-h2">What this lens said at past extremes</h2>
    <p class="dp-lead">Every figure below is computed by the same code that drives the slider above, run against the market&rsquo;s four cyclical tops and three cyclical lows. <strong>The same arithmetic that reads high today read deeply negative at every major top &mdash; and strongly positive at every major low.</strong> The same formula, at every extreme.</p>

    <div class="dp-table-wrap" data-chart-copy data-chart-heading="#backtest .dp-h2" data-chart-sub="#backtest .dp-lead" data-chart-title="What reverting to trend implied at bitcoin's cyclical tops" data-chart-filename="bitcoin-reversion-cagr-backtest.png">
      <table class="dp-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" rowspan="2">Moment</th>
            <th scope="col" rowspan="2">Multiple of trend</th>
            <th class="dp-th-span" colspan="3">Implied CAGR if trend is reached within&hellip;</th>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" title="Annualized return if price moved from this moment&rsquo;s price to the trend line within 1 year.">1&nbsp;year</th>
            <th scope="col" title="Annualized return if price moved from this moment&rsquo;s price to the trend line within 2 years.">2&nbsp;years</th>
            <th scope="col" title="Annualized return if price moved from this moment&rsquo;s price to the trend line within 3 years.">3&nbsp;years</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="dpBacktestBody"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="dp-table-example">Read it like this: buying the Dec 2017 top meant even a full reversion within a year implied &minus;71%/yr; buying the Dec 2018 low, the same reversion implied +205%/yr.</p>
    <p class="dp-table-note">Implied CAGR of a return to trend from each moment, at one-, two- and three-year horizons. Top and low anchors are the site&rsquo;s reference cyclical-extreme set, shared with <a href="/bitcoin-vs-the-stock-market">Bitcoin vs. The Stock Market</a> and <a href="/bull-and-bear-cycles">Bull &amp; Bear Cycles</a>; multiples and rates are computed from the model, not asserted.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════ WHY CAGR FALLS OVER TIME ════════ -->
  <section class="dp-section" id="declining">
    <div class="dp-eyebrow">Before you extrapolate</div>
    <h2 class="dp-h2">Why the baseline itself keeps falling</h2>
    <p class="dp-lead">The at-trend baseline above is not a constant. The Power Law describes growth that is <em>proportional but decelerating</em> &mdash; each doubling takes longer than the last &mdash; so the trend&rsquo;s own annual rate declines by construction, whatever price does around it. Taking today&rsquo;s number and extending it across decades overstates the case badly.</p>
    <div class="dp-slope-grid" id="dpSlopeGrid"></div>
    <p class="dp-table-note" style="margin-top:0.9rem;">Annualised growth of the trend line itself across successive four-year windows, computed live. This is the rate you earn if the multiple never changes &mdash; and it is the reason a single &ldquo;bitcoin&rsquo;s CAGR&rdquo; figure is always incomplete without a date attached. For the trend structure behind it, see <a href="/the-doubling-ladder">The Doubling Ladder</a>.</p>
  </section>

